    <!-- Datatypes of metadata files -->
    <token name="@METADATA_FORMATS@">yaml,json,jsonld</token>
    <xml name="requirements">
        <requirements>
            <!-- the Bioconda package includes pyld (the jsonld extra) from 0.8.2 build 0 -->
            <requirement type="package" version="@TOOL_VERSION@">fair-bioheaders</requirement>
        </requirements>
    </xml>
    <xml name="xrefs">
        <xrefs>
            <xref type="bio.tools">fair-bioheaders</xref>
        </xrefs>
    </xml>
    <xml name="version_command">
        <version_command>bioheaders --version</version_command>
    </xml>
    <xml name="stdio">
        <stdio>
            <!-- bioheaders reports every error, including an uncaught exception, with a nonzero exit status -->
            <exit_code range="1:" level="fatal"/>
        </stdio>
    </xml>
    <xml name="edam_topics">
        <edam_topics>
            <edam_topic>topic_0219</edam_topic>
            <edam_topic>topic_0080</edam_topic>
        </edam_topics>
    </xml>
    <!-- Defines base_type($dataset): the bioheaders format of a dataset (yaml, json,
         jsonld, fasta, gfa or gff3), also for datatypes derived from these, such as
         a2m or freq.json, whose extensions bioheaders doesn't know. Jsonld is
         derived from json, so it is checked first. Compression is detected from the
         content, so .gz datasets need no option. -->
    <token name="@BASE_TYPE@"><![CDATA[
#def base_type($dataset)
    #if $dataset.is_of_type('jsonld')
        #return 'jsonld'
    #elif $dataset.is_of_type('json')
        #return 'json'
    #elif $dataset.is_of_type('yaml')
        #return 'yaml'
    #elif $dataset.is_of_type('gff3', 'gff3.gz')
        #return 'gff3'
    #elif $dataset.is_of_type('gfa1', 'gfa2', 'gfa1.gz', 'gfa2.gz')
        #return 'gfa'
    #else
        #return 'fasta'
    #end if
#end def
    ]]></token>
    <!-- Sets $type and $compressed for the dataset $input. bioheaders writes BGZF
         to an output path ending in .gz, so $out is named to keep compression. -->
    <token name="@SET_TYPE@"><![CDATA[
@BASE_TYPE@
#set $type = $base_type($input)
#set $compressed = $input.is_of_type('fasta.gz', 'gfa1.gz', 'gfa2.gz', 'gff3.gz')
    ]]></token>
    <token name="@HEADER_HELP@"><![CDATA[
FAIR-bioHeaders (FHR, see the `specification`_) puts provenance metadata in a header at the top of a
data file: who made the genome assembly, annotation or protein set, from
which organism, version, accession, license, the files it was derived from, and
a checksum. The header lines start with ``;~`` in FASTA
and ``#~`` in GFA and GFF3, before the first record, so the data itself is not
changed. Headers follow the FHR schema for genome assemblies, FHP for protein
FASTA and FHGFF3 for GFF3 annotation.

The checksum (SHA-512/256 of the whole file, header included, with a
placeholder in place of the checksum itself) lets anyone check that neither
the data nor the metadata has changed. Any change to the sequence,
annotation or metadata needs a new checksum: recombine the file.
    ]]></token>
    <token name="@GALAXY_HELP@"><![CDATA[
**Datatypes**

Galaxy's FASTA datatype expects ``>`` on the first line, so a FASTA file that
starts with a header may not be detected as FASTA on upload: set its datatype
to ``fasta`` (or ``fasta.gz``) by hand. Most tools read headed files without
problems; for one that doesn't, remove the header with the
**FAIR-bioHeaders strip** tool first.

Compressed (``.gz``) inputs give compressed outputs, written as BGZF (a
form of gzip that htslib tools can index).
    ]]></token>
    <token name="@LINKS@"><![CDATA[
.. _specification: https://github.com/FAIR-bioHeaders/FAIR-bioHeaders-Specification
.. _fair-bioheaders: https://github.com/FAIR-bioHeaders/FAIR-bioHeaders-Tools
    ]]></token>
